{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; .venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('STATE.md');s=p.read_text();a='<\"'!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:resource_finder -->'\"';b='<\"'!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:resource_finder -->'\"';note='''\nPhase 3 resource acquisition and code inspection complete: pinned simple-evals and sparse inspect_evals clones, four model metadata snapshots, exact 50/400 split and 150 controls. See artifacts/resource_finder/{code_revisions,model_revisions,item_manifest,preparation_validation}.json and scripts/prepare_simpleqa.py. SimpleQA SHA matches upstream; all 4,326 IDs unique. Reference parsers silently map judge parse failure to abstention: runner must override with unscored. Baseline is raw question in both pinned implementations. Native DeepSeek template already starts <think>; Qwen thinking disabled via template kwargs. Paper-finder timed out after 300s (paper_finder.json); five relevant PDFs obtained by manual source discovery. Next Phase 4: finalize literature review, resource/readme catalogs, preregistration handoff and validation. GPU inference, judge eligibility and budget headroom are untested runner responsibilities.\n''';p.write_text(s.split(a)[0]+a+note+b+s.split(b)[1])\nPY\ncat STATE.md\ncat > datasets/download.sh <<'SH'\n#\"'!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\npwd\ntest -d .git\ntest -x .venv/bin/python\n.venv/bin/python scripts/prepare_simpleqa.py\nSH\ncat > datasets/README.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# SimpleQA data\n\nThe mandated dataset is the exact OpenAI SimpleQA CSV used by the pinned Inspect task (\"'`code/inspect_evals/src/inspect_evals/simpleqa/dataset.py`). Full data are locally available and excluded from git; documentation, this script and a three-record sample are retained.\n\n- Source: `https://openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net/simple-evals/simple_qa_test_set.csv`\n- SHA256: `feee3f7e7db3617e94e8fcf1977b756ec420ef8568f4e0fcbbe0e92e9d5fc032`\n- Size: 2,012,910 bytes; 4,326 examples; English, short fact-seeking QA.\n- Format: UTF-8 CSV with `metadata`, `problem`, `answer`. Metadata is a Python-literal dictionary, **not JSON**; parse with `ast.literal_eval`, never `eval`.\n- Split: one evaluation set; no training split. Our deterministic 450-item selection reserves 50 for calibration and 400 for main evaluation.\n- License: official simple-evals README associates SimpleQA with MIT; retain its LICENSE. The HF mirror card also lists MIT.\n- HF source checked first: `basicv8vc/SimpleQA`, revision `e319282ab125c3dbd0c7fd00be2e4dd54e7e8f94`; current pinned Inspect uses the official blob, so the mirror is not substituted. Its bytes were not assumed equivalent.\n\n## Reproduction from workspace root\n\n```bash\npwd\nuv sync --project .\nbash datasets/download.sh\n```\n\nThe downloader obtains the official CSV if absent, verifies the frozen SHA256, derives canonical IDs with the pinned Inspect helper, checks all IDs/questions for uniqueness, and recreates the data below. Restore the pinned code checkout first if needed (see `code/README.md`). An unexpected source hash fails instead of silently changing the sample.\n\n| Local file | Records | Purpose |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `simple_qa_test_set.csv` | 4,326 | Original full benchmark |\n| `calibration_questions.jsonl` | 50 | Disjoint control questions only |\n| `main_questions.jsonl` | 400 | Same evaluation items for all conditions, models and seeds |\n| `calibration_controls.jsonl` | 150 | Exact gold / explicit abstention / explicit gold-negation controls, 50 per label |\n| `examples.json` | 3 | Small documentation sample from full dataset |\n\nManifest: `artifacts/resource_finder/item_manifest.json`. Canonical IDs follow Inspect'\"'s \"'`simpleqa_` plus first eight hex characters of MD5(question + NUL + answer). Sort by SHA256 of UTF-8 `20261020:` plus canonical ID; use ranks 0–49 for calibration, 50–449 for main. Source row indices are zero-based excluding the CSV header. Delimiter and serialization are frozen explicitly.\n\n## Loading\n\n```python\nimport csv, json, ast\nwith open('\"'datasets/simple_qa_test_set.csv', newline='') as f:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(f))\nmetadata = ast.literal_eval(rows[0]['metadata'])\nwith open('datasets/main_questions.jsonl') as f:\n    main = [json.loads(line) for line in f]\n\"'```\n\nExample: “Who received the IEEE Frank Rosenblatt Award in 2010?” → “Michio Sugeno”. Full samples with source URLs are in `examples.json`.\n\n## Validation and limitations\n\nAll 4,326 questions and question-answer pairs are unique; no empty questions or answers. Metadata parsed successfully across all rows. All IDs are unique; calibration and main sets are disjoint. Evidence: `dataset_validation.json` and `preparation_validation.json` in `artifacts/resource_finder/`.\n\nSynthetic controls are labelled relative to supplied gold and are not human-labelled natural model completions. The contradiction is `The answer to this question is not \"<gold>\".` It tests whether judges recognize negation, including when the gold string appears. SimpleQA'\"'s original audit estimates about 3% label error; semantic redundancy can remain despite exact-string uniqueness. Do not use metadata URLs as retrieval input to subjects. Do not replace this benchmark with SimpleQA Verified or silently repair gold answers after seeing outputs.\nEOF\ncat > code/README.md <<'EOF'\n# Evaluation code resources\n\nTwo repositories cloned; no subject or judge inference has been run. Commit pins are also machine-readable in \"'`artifacts/resource_finder/code_revisions.json`.\n\n## OpenAI simple-evals\n\n- Source: `https://github.com/openai/simple-evals.git`\n- Local: `code/simple-evals/`\n- Commit: `652c89d0ca9df547706735883097e9537d40dc47`\n- MIT; deprecated for new benchmark/model updates since July 2025, retained as the reference implementation.\n- Key file: `simpleqa_eval.py`, especially `GRADER_TEMPLATE`, `grade_sample`, `__call__` and aggregate metrics. `sampler/chat_completion_sampler.py` supplies API sampling; `common.py` aggregation.\n- README inspected in full. Dependencies are optional/per-eval rather than one maintained install: pandas, numpy, scipy, jinja2, tqdm, requests, openai/anthropic as applicable. Do not run its default API model sweep for this study.\n- The subject message is the raw question, with no extra short-answer instruction. We preserve that baseline and append the exact requested suffix only in treatment.\n- Parser uses the first A/B/C character anywhere and falls back to C on failure. Reuse the **rubric**, not this fallback. Our frozen grader is copied verbatim to `artifacts/resource_finder/grader_prompt.txt`.\n\n## Inspect Evals\n\n- Source: `https://github.com/UKGovernmentBEIS/inspect_evals.git`\n- Local: `code/inspect_evals/` (sparse checkout)\n- Commit: `8ef810f09930362302fc59b10a382d96dd767171`\n- MIT. Checked-out paths: `src/inspect_evals/simpleqa`, `src/inspect_evals/utils`, `tests/simpleqa`, plus top-level files and package parent files.\n- Task: `src/inspect_evals/simpleqa/simpleqa.py`; data/hash and record mapping: `dataset.py`; rubric: `grader_templates.py`; scoring: `scorer.py`; stable ID helper: `src/inspect_evals/utils/deps_utils.py`.\n- Root and task README/dependency declarations inspected. Pinned requirements include Python >=3.10, inspect_ai >=0.3.261, datasets >=4.8.5, huggingface_hub >=1.2.0, numpy, pydantic, requests, jinja2 and tiktoken. Paper config mentions inspect_ai >=0.3.221, but use the stricter project dependency floor. Version compatibility must be checked when installing the GPU runner.\n- `simpleqa` now defaults to a tool-calling scorer. Original option uses a standalone-token regex in strict mode but **still maps unmatched outputs to C**. Use a custom scorer preserving unscored cases, explicit judge assignment and blind original question; never let the tested model become the fallback grader.\n- Native metrics can exclude failures;