. SimpleQA SHA matches upstream; all 4,326 IDs unique. Reference parsers silently map judge parse failure to abstention: runner must override with unscored. Baseline is raw question in both pinned implementations. Native DeepSeek template already starts <think>; Qwen thinking disabled via template kwargs. Paper-finder timed out after 300s (paper_finder.json); five relevant PDFs obtained by manual source discovery. Next Phase 4: finalize literature review, resource/readme catalogs, preregistration handoff and validation. GPU inference, judge eligibility and budget headroom are untested runner responsibilities.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:resource_finder -->\n\n### Legacy notes\nNo phase notes recorded yet.\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END -->\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; cat >> literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n## Search and screening\n\nSearch date: October 20, 2026. Primary paper-finder diligent query (above) timed out after its 300-second timeout and returned no papers; saved in \"'`artifacts/resource_finder/paper_finder.json`. Web search calls yielded no usable source text. Manual discovery used arXiv papers, reference chasing from SimpleQA, the official GitHub implementations, and HF dataset/model cards. Five targeted candidates were downloaded, all five included; this is a focused review, not an exhaustive systematic search. No citation-count claims are made. Raw PDF hashes and URLs: `artifacts/resource_finder/paper_downloads.json`.\n\nSimpleQA was the deep-read benchmark paper: all five chunk PDFs (13 pages) were inspected, including rendered tables/equations, with notes above. Other papers received abstract/method/result screening, not full appendix review. Chunk manifests exist for all five so later readers can continue without assuming unread material was reviewed.\n\n## Key papers\n\n**1. Wei, Jason; Nguyen, Karina; Chung, Hyung Won; Jiao, Yunxin Joy; Papay, Spencer; Glaese, Amelia; Schulman, John; Fedus, William (2024). Measuring short-form factuality in large language models.** arXiv:2411.04368v1. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04368`. Local: `papers/2411.04368_simpleqa.pdf`.\n\nThe central contribution is a challenging 4,326-item factuality benchmark with correct/incorrect/not-attempted grading. Historical GPT/Claude baselines differ substantially in willingness to answer. Method, numerical examples and caveats are documented chunk by chunk above. Official code: `https://github.com/openai/simple-evals` (cloned). The paper motivates our estimands and rubric, but provides no evidence for the exact Qwen3 0.10/0.03 joint threshold. Its informal judge spot check is not calibration of our two judges.\n\n**2. Kirichenko, Polina; Ibrahim, Mark; Chaudhuri, Kamalika; Bell, Samuel J. (2025). AbstentionBench: Reasoning LLMs Fail on Unanswerable Questions.** arXiv:2506.09038v1. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09038`. Local: `papers/2506.09038_abstentionbench.pdf`.\n\nCurates 20 datasets covering six scenarios (unknown answers, false premises, stale information, subjectivity, underspecified context/intent), including modified GSM8K/GPQA/MMLU and established QA datasets. Evaluates 20 LLMs and post-training/reasoning variants, with abstention precision/recall/F1 and a separate correctness judge. A Llama-3.1-8B abstention judge achieved 88% agreement on their manually annotated sample (p. 5). The abstract reports reasoning fine-tuning degrading abstention by 24% on average; sections 4.3–4.4 discuss reasoning and prompting. Stronger prompting can help, but no universal model-size advantage appears. This is evidence that a prompt experiment is plausible and model-specific, not evidence that DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B must behave the same way as their larger checkpoints. Their binary abstention definition and unanswerable questions differ from SimpleQA'\"'s three-class factuality task. Code: \"'`https://github.com/facebookresearch/AbstentionBench` (source tree inspected for annotation resources; not cloned because the full suite was pruned).\n\n**3. Zhang, Hanning; Diao, Shizhe; Lin, Yong; Fung, Yi R.; Lian, Qing; Wang, Xingyao; Chen, Yangyi; Ji, Heng; Zhang, Tong (2023; revised 2024). R-Tuning: Instructing Large Language Models to Say ‘I Don’t Know’.** arXiv:2311.09677v3. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.09677`. Local: `papers/2311.09677_r_tuning.pdf`.\n\nIdentifies known/unknown training questions by model-answer agreement, then teaches uncertainty alongside answers. An unsupervised variant uses consistency across sampled answers. Nine datasets include ParaRel, MMLU, HotpotQA, HaluEval, SelfAware, FalseQA, NEC, WiCE and FEVER. Baselines are original pretrained checkpoints, those checkpoints evaluated on willingly answered items, and vanilla instruction tuning; models include OpenLLaMA-3B and LLaMA-7B/13B. Uses answered-subset accuracy and average precision. Table 1 gives LLaMA-13B out-of-domain ParaRel AP 77.30 versus 64.12 for vanilla tuning; not every model/task improves. This motivates measuring both selection quality and answer coverage. It does **not** establish that a one-sentence inference intervention will reproduce training effects. Code: `https://github.com/shizhediao/R-Tuning` (not cloned; training excluded).\n\n**4. Kadavath, Saurav, et al. (2022). Language Models (Mostly) Know What They Know.** arXiv:2207.05221v4. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.05221`. Local: `papers/2207.05221_know_what_they_know.pdf`.\n\nStudies self-evaluated P(True) of proposed answers and trained P(IK) of knowing an answer. Multiple-choice/true-false benchmarks and generative tasks include TriviaQA, Lambada, GSM8K, arithmetic and code. Compares size, few-shot format, sample context and trained knowledge predictors; evaluates calibration and discrimination, including AUROC. Larger models and appropriate formats improve self-evaluation, while P(IK) generalization remains imperfectly calibrated out of distribution. This supplies a mechanism for selective answering but neither proves introspection nor guarantees that a literal uncertainty instruction induces calibrated decisions. Public complete training/checkpoint reproduction was not established in this resource search. Confidence elicitation is excluded as an additional treatment.\n\n**5. Haas, Lukas; Yona, Gal; D’Antonio, Giovanni; Goldshtein, Sasha; Das, Dipanjan (2025; revised March 10, 2026). SimpleQA Verified: A Reliable Factuality Benchmark to Measure Parametric Knowledge.** arXiv:2509.07968v2. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07968`. Local: `papers/2509.07968_simpleqa_verified.pdf`.\n\nProduces 1,000 prompts by deduplication, balancing, source checks, ambiguity removal and difficulty filtering. Retains the three-way grading/F1 framework while refining the autorater for elaborate answers, approximate numeric responses and soft refusals. Reviews disagreement-prone outputs obtained from ten high-temperature grader repetitions. The abstract reports Gemini-2.5-Pro F1 55.6 on this new benchmark; this is not comparable to original SimpleQA scores. Filtering also considers publisher training restrictions and frontier-model difficulty, so the subset changes more than label noise alone. Data/evaluation release: `https://www.kaggle.com/benchmarks/deepmind/simpleqa-verified`; Inspect has a separate task. This motivates explicit label-error and judge-domain-shift limitations. We do not substitute Verified or add another dataset experiment.\n\n## Synthesis and experiment recommendations\n\nThe papers distinguish three approaches: direct permission to abstain (our treatment), post-generation confidence filtering (Kadavath), and training to express uncertainty (R-Tuning). They also distinguish an answerable question the model does not know (SimpleQA) from an intrinsically unanswerable or underspecified question (AbstentionBench). These distinctions rule out interpreting our abstention rate as a safety-refusal metric.\n\nUse the **raw-question reference baseline** confirmed in both cloned implementations and append only the fixed suffix. Historical published scores are context, not target baselines. No retrieved evidence resolves the size of this intervention'\"'s effect on the three specified checkpoints. The study's value is the paired effect and its correct-answer retention, not demonstrating that abstention can trivially lower error by suppressing all answers.\n\nReport correct C/N, incorrect I/N and not-attempted A/N; coverage (C+I)/N; attempted accuracy C/(C+I); and native F = 2C/(2C+2I+A) for fully scored data. Invalid/unscored cases have their own fields and remain in the main N=400 denominator after averaging three seeds per item. Explicit literal abstention should additionally be tracked separately from semantic NOT_ATTEMPTED. A confidence interval crossing the retention margin is inconclusive, not evidence of equivalence.\n\nSynthetic calibration checks that each judge recognizes gold, negation and abstention. It does not test accuracy on the natural distribution of hedges, partial answers and hallucinations. No task-matched public human-labelled model-completion calibration set was located in the reviewed simple-evals/Inspect files or the inspected AbstentionBench source-tree paths; that limited search is not proof none exists. The missing shared \"'`evals/judge-calibration.md` supplies no evidence. Require the staged eligibility thresholds and report the domain-shift caveat even when both judges pass.\n\nFrozen design details, failure gates, seed handling and bounds are in `preregistration.md`; resource acquisition details are in `resources.md`. Preserve the three-direction scope recorded in `planning.md`.\nEOF\ncat > papers/README.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Downloaded papers\n\nFive original PDFs; PDF chunks in \"'`pages/` are derivatives and are not counted as additional papers. All PDFs were successfully parsed by pypdf. Source URLs, sizes and SHA256 hashes are in `../artifacts/resource_finder/paper_downloads.json`. URLs without version suffix may change; the local hashes and versions below identify what was actually read.\n\n| Paper / authors | Version and year | Local PDF | Why relevant / reading depth |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Measuring short-form factuality in large language models — Jason Wei, Karina Nguyen, Hyung Won Chung, Yunxin Joy Jiao, Spencer Papay, Amelia Glaese, John Schulman, William Fedus | 2411.04368v1, 2024 | `2411.04368_simpleqa.pdf` | Exact benchmark, rubric, coverage metrics; all 13 pages / 5 chunks read |\n| AbstentionBench: Reasoning LLMs Fail on Unanswerable Questions — Polina Kirichenko, Mark Ibrahim, Kamalika Chaudhuri, Samuel J. Bell | 2506.09038v1, 2025 | `2506.09038_abstentionbench.pdf` | Prompting/reasoning and judge limitations; abstract/method/results screen |\n| R-Tuning: Instructing Large Language Models to Say ‘I Don’t Know’ — Hanning Zhang, Shizhe Diao, Yong Lin, Yi R. Fung, Qing Lian, Xingyao Wang, Yangyi Chen, Heng Ji, Tong Zhang | 2311.09677v3, 2023/revised 2024 | `2311.09677_r_tuning.pdf` | Training alternative and selective metrics; abstract/method/results screen |\n| Language Models (Mostly) Know What They Know — Saurav Kadavath et al. | 2207.05221v4, 2022 | `2207.05221_know_what_they_know.pdf` | Calibration foundation; abstract/contribution/results overview screen |\n| SimpleQA Verified: A Reliable Factuality Benchmark to Measure Parametric Knowledge — Lukas Haas, Gal Yona, Giovanni D’Antonio, Sasha Goldshtein, Dipanjan Das | 2509.07968v2, 2025/revised 2026 | `2509.07968_simpleqa_verified.pdf` | Reference error, redundancy, autorater weaknesses; abstract/method/results screen |\n\nSources: arXiv abstract/PDF endpoints using the IDs above. Detailed source links and synthesis are in `../literature_review.md`. All five screened candidates were relevant. The paper-finder service timed out without returning ranked papers, so there are no omitted paper-finder results.\n\nTo recreate chunks from workspace root:\n\n```bash\npwd\n.venv/bin/python .codex/skills/paper-finder/scripts/pdf_chunker.py papers/2411.04368_simpleqa.pdf --pages-per-chunk 3 --output-dir papers/pages\n```\n\nEach manifest lists every chunk and page range. SimpleQA rendered chunk previews are in `../artifacts/resource_finder/`. Other papers'\"' appendices have not been fully reviewed; do not assume full-paper coverage from chunk creation alone.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"pwd; cat >> literature_review.md <<'EOF'\n## Search and screening\n\nSearch date: October 20, 2026. Primary paper-finder diligent query (above) timed out after its 300-second timeout and returned no papers; saved in \"'`artifacts/resource_finder/paper_finder.json`. Web search calls yielded no usable source text. Manual discovery used arXiv papers, reference chasing from SimpleQA, the official GitHub implementations, and HF dataset/model cards. Five targeted candidates were downloaded, all five included; this is a focused review, not an exhaustive systematic search. No citation-count claims are made. Raw PDF hashes and URLs: `artifacts/resource_finder/paper_downloads.json`.\n\nSimpleQA was the deep-read benchmark paper: all five chunk PDFs (13 pages) were inspected, including rendered tables/equations, with notes above. Other papers received abstract/method/result screening, not full appendix review. Chunk manifests exist for all five so later readers can continue without assuming unread material was reviewed.\n\n## Key papers\n\n**1. Wei, Jason; Nguyen, Karina; Chung, Hyung Won; Jiao, Yunxin Joy; Papay, Spencer; Glaese, Amelia; Schulman, John; Fedus, William (2024). Measuring short-form factuality in large language models.** arXiv:2411.04368v1. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.04368`. Local: `papers/2411.04368_simpleqa.pdf`.\n\nThe central contribution is a challenging 4,326-item factuality benchmark with correct/incorrect/not-attempted grading. Historical GPT/Claude baselines differ substantially in willingness to answer. Method, numerical examples and caveats are documented chunk by chunk above. Official code: `https://github.com/openai/simple-evals` (cloned). The paper motivates our estimands and rubric, but provides no evidence for the exact Qwen3 0.10/0.03 joint threshold. Its informal judge spot check is not calibration of our two judges.\n\n**2. Kirichenko, Polina; Ibrahim, Mark; Chaudhuri, Kamalika; Bell, Samuel J. (2025). AbstentionBench: Reasoning LLMs Fail on Unanswerable Questions.** arXiv:2506.09038v1. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.09038`. Local: `papers/2506.09038_abstentionbench.pdf`.\n\nCurates 20 datasets covering six scenarios (unknown answers, false premises, stale information, subjectivity, underspecified context/intent), including modified GSM8K/GPQA/MMLU and established QA datasets. Evaluates 20 LLMs and post-training/reasoning variants, with abstention precision/recall/F1 and a separate correctness judge. A Llama-3.1-8B abstention judge achieved 88% agreement on their manually annotated sample (p. 5). The abstract reports reasoning fine-tuning degrading abstention by 24% on average; sections 4.3–4.4 discuss reasoning and prompting. Stronger prompting can help, but no universal model-size advantage appears. This is evidence that a prompt experiment is plausible and model-specific, not evidence that DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B must behave the same way as their larger checkpoints. Their binary abstention definition and unanswerable questions differ from SimpleQA'\"'s three-class factuality task. Code: \"'`https://github.com/facebookresearch/AbstentionBench` (source tree inspected for annotation resources; not cloned because the full suite was pruned).\n\n**3. Zhang, Hanning; Diao, Shizhe; Lin, Yong; Fung, Yi R.; Lian, Qing; Wang, Xingyao; Chen, Yangyi; Ji, Heng; Zhang, Tong (2023; revised 2024). R-Tuning: Instructing Large Language Models to Say ‘I Don’t Know’.** arXiv:2311.09677v3. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.09677`. Local: `papers/2311.09677_r_tuning.pdf`.\n\nIdentifies known/unknown training questions by model-answer agreement, then teaches uncertainty alongside answers. An unsupervised variant uses consistency across sampled answers. Nine datasets include ParaRel, MMLU, HotpotQA, HaluEval, SelfAware, FalseQA, NEC, WiCE and FEVER. Baselines are original pretrained checkpoints, those checkpoints evaluated on willingly answered items, and vanilla instruction tuning; models include OpenLLaMA-3B and LLaMA-7B/13B. Uses answered-subset accuracy and average precision. Table 1 gives LLaMA-13B out-of-domain ParaRel AP 77.30 versus 64.12 for vanilla tuning; not every model/task improves. This motivates measuring both selection quality and answer coverage. It does **not** establish that a one-sentence inference intervention will reproduce training effects. Code: `https://github.com/shizhediao/R-Tuning` (not cloned; training excluded).\n\n**4. Kadavath, Saurav, et al. (2022). Language Models (Mostly) Know What They Know.** arXiv:2207.05221v4. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.05221`. Local: `papers/2207.05221_know_what_they_know.pdf`.\n\nStudies self-evaluated P(True) of proposed answers and trained P(IK) of knowing an answer. Multiple-choice/true-false benchmarks and generative tasks include TriviaQA, Lambada, GSM8K, arithmetic and code. Compares size, few-shot format, sample context and trained knowledge predictors; evaluates calibration and discrimination, including AUROC. Larger models and appropriate formats improve self-evaluation, while P(IK) generalization remains imperfectly calibrated out of distribution. This supplies a mechanism for selective answering but neither proves introspection nor guarantees that a literal uncertainty instruction induces calibrated decisions. Public complete training/checkpoint reproduction was not established in this resource search. Confidence elicitation is excluded as an additional treatment.\n\n**5. Haas, Lukas; Yona, Gal; D’Antonio, Giovanni; Goldshtein, Sasha; Das, Dipanjan (2025; revised March 10, 2026). SimpleQA Verified: A Reliable Factuality Benchmark to Measure Parametric Knowledge.** arXiv:2509.07968v2. Source: `https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07968`. Local: `papers/2509.07968_simpleqa_verified.pdf`.\n\nProduces 1,000 prompts by deduplication, balancing, source checks, ambiguity removal and difficulty filtering. Retains the three-way grading/F1 framework while refining the autorater for elaborate answers, approximate numeric responses and soft refusals. Reviews disagreement-prone outputs obtained from ten high-temperature grader repetitions. The abstract reports Gemini-2.5-Pro F1 55.6 on this new benchmark; this is not comparable to original SimpleQA scores. Filtering also considers publisher training restrictions and frontier-model difficulty, so the subset changes more than label noise alone. Data/evaluation release: `https://www.kaggle.com/benchmarks/deepmind/simpleqa-verified`; Inspect has a separate task. This motivates explicit label-error and judge-domain-shift limitations. We do not substitute Verified or add another dataset experiment.\n\n## Synthesis and experiment recommendations\n\nThe papers distinguish three approaches: direct permission to abstain (our treatment), post-generation confidence filtering (Kadavath), and training to express uncertainty (R-Tuning). They also distinguish an answerable question the model does not know (SimpleQA) from an intrinsically unanswerable or underspecified question (AbstentionBench). These distinctions rule out interpreting our abstention rate as a safety-refusal metric.\n\nUse the **raw-question reference baseline** confirmed in both cloned implementations and append only the fixed suffix. Historical published scores are context, not target baselines. No retrieved evidence resolves the size of this intervention'\"'s effect on the three specified checkpoints. The study's value is the paired effect and its correct-answer retention, not demonstrating that abstention can trivially lower error by suppressing all answers.\n\nReport correct C/N, incorrect I/N and not-attempted A/N; coverage (C+I)/N; attempted accuracy C/(C+I); and native F = 2C/(2C+2I+A) for fully scored data. Invalid/unscored cases have their own fields and remain in the main N=400 denominator after averaging three seeds per item. Explicit literal abstention should additionally be tracked separately from semantic NOT_ATTEMPTED. A confidence interval crossing the retention margin is inconclusive, not evidence of equivalence.\n\nSynthetic calibration checks that each judge recognizes gold, negation and abstention. It does not test accuracy on the natural distribution of hedges, partial answers and hallucinations. No task-matched public human-labelled model-completion calibration set was located in the reviewed simple-evals/Inspect files or the inspected AbstentionBench source-tree paths; that limited search is not proof none exists. The missing shared \"'`evals/judge-calibration.md` supplies no evidence. Require the staged eligibility thresholds and report the domain-shift caveat even when both judges pass.\n\nFrozen design details, failure gates, seed handling and bounds are in `preregistration.md`; resource acquisition details are in `resources.md`. Preserve the three-direction scope recorded in `planning.md`.\nEOF\ncat > papers/README.md <<'\"'EOF'\n# Downloaded papers\n\nFive original PDFs; PDF chunks in \"'`pages/` are derivatives and are not counted as additional papers. All PDFs were successfully parsed by pypdf. Source URLs, sizes and SHA256 hashes are in `../artifacts/resource_finder/paper_downloads.json`. URLs without version suffix may change; the local hashes and versions below identify what was actually read.\n\n| Paper / authors | Version and year | Local PDF | Why relevant / reading depth |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Measuring short-form factuality in large language models — Jason Wei, Karina Nguyen, Hyung Won Chung, Yunxin Joy Jiao, Spencer Papay, Amelia Glaese, John Schulman, William Fedus | 2411.04368v1, 2024 | `2411.04368_simpleqa.pdf` | Exact benchmark, rubric, coverage metrics; all 13 pages / 5 chunks read |\n| AbstentionBench: Reasoning LLMs Fail on Unanswerable Questions — Polina Kirichenko, Mark Ibrahim, Kamalika Chaudhuri, Samuel J. Bell | 2506.09038v1, 2025 | `2506.09038_abstentionbench.pdf` | Prompting/reasoning and judge limitations; abstract/method/results screen |\n| R-Tuning: Instructing Large Language Models to Say ‘I Don’t Know’ — Hanning Zhang, Shizhe Diao, Yong Lin, Yi R. Fung, Qing Lian, Xingyao Wang, Yangyi Chen, Heng Ji, Tong Zhang | 2311.09677v3, 2023/revised 2024 | `2311.09677_r_tuning.pdf` | Training alternative and selective metrics; abstract/method/results screen |\n| Language Models (Mostly) Know What They Know — Saurav Kadavath et al. | 2207.05221v4, 2022 | `2207.05221_know_what_they_know.pdf` | Calibration foundation; abstract/contribution/results overview screen |\n| SimpleQA Verified: A Reliable Factuality Benchmark to Measure Parametric Knowledge — Lukas Haas, Gal Yona, Giovanni D’Antonio, Sasha Goldshtein, Dipanjan Das | 2509.07968v2, 2025/revised 2026 | `2509.07968_simpleqa_verified.pdf` | Reference error, redundancy, autorater weaknesses; abstract/method/results screen |\n\nSources: arXiv abstract/PDF endpoints using the IDs above. Detailed source links and synthesis are in `../literature_review.md`. All five screened candidates were relevant. The paper-finder service timed out without returning ranked papers, so there are no omitted paper-finder results.\n\nTo recreate chunks from workspace root:\n\n```bash\npwd\n.venv/bin/python .codex/skills/paper-finder/scripts/pdf_chunker.py papers/2411.04368_simpleqa.pdf --pages-per-chunk 3 --output-dir papers/pages\n```\n\nEach manifest lists every chunk and page range. SimpleQA rendered chunk previews are in `../artifacts/resource_finder/`. Other papers'\"' appendices have not been fully reviewed; do not assume full-paper coverage from chunk creation alone.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"/net/spaces/chenhao/chai-lab-models/haokunliu/safety/workspaces/simpleqa-factual-errors-versus-e11f-codex-gpt-6-astra\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
